<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist and how it is diagnosed</h2>
<p>See an oral and maxillofacial surgeon, an oral medicine specialist or your dentist if you chew areca nut in any form and notice burning with spicy food, a pale or stiff lining, or a mouth that seems to open less than it used to. Early assessment gives the most room to prevent further stiffening.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Do not wait</strong> if you have an ulcer that has not healed within two weeks, a white or red patch, a lump, bleeding or numbness in the mouth, a lump in the neck, a sudden drop in mouth opening or difficulty swallowing. These need prompt examination to rule out cancer. Read about the <a href="/blogs/what-are-the-symptoms-of-oral-cancer/">symptoms of oral cancer</a>.</p>
</div>
<p>Diagnosis is mainly clinical: a history of areca nut use, the typical burning and blanching, and the bands felt on examination. Mouth opening is measured and recorded so that changes can be tracked over time. A <strong>biopsy</strong> under local anaesthetic is advised if there is any ulcer, patch or area that looks suspicious, and sometimes to confirm the diagnosis. Blood tests may be used to check for anaemia and vitamin deficiencies.</p>

<h2 id="treatment">Treatment: what can and cannot be achieved</h2>
<p>No treatment can reliably reverse established fibrosis, so the realistic goals are to stop progression, relieve symptoms, improve mouth opening and watch carefully for cancer. The evidence behind many treatments is of modest quality, so plans are tailored to the individual and the stage of disease.</p>
<h3>1. Stop areca nut completely</h3>
<p>This is the single most important step, and it applies to every form — supari, gutka, pan masala, mawa and paan. Stopping may halt progression and ease burning, and it lowers the cancer risk. Stopping tobacco and alcohol matters too. Cutting down is less effective than stopping, and switching to "tobacco-free" pan masala does not help, because the areca nut is the problem. Support from your doctor, counselling and the national quitline number printed on tobacco packets can all help.</p>
<h3>2. Mouth-opening physiotherapy</h3>
<p>Regular stretching exercises are central to treatment at every stage and essential after any surgery. Your team will show you how to do them safely, but they typically include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Gradually stacking wooden spatulas or ice-cream sticks between the front teeth, adding one more as opening improves</li>
  <li>Using a mouth-opening device or exerciser as directed</li>
  <li>Cheek stretches, tongue movements and blowing exercises</li>
  <li>Warm salt-water rinses before exercising to ease stiffness</li>
</ul>
<p>Exercises work only if they are done several times a day, every day, for months. Gains are lost if they are stopped too soon.</p>
<h3>3. Medical treatment</h3>
<p>Options your specialist may discuss include avoiding spicy and very hot food and using soothing gels for burning; correcting anaemia or vitamin deficiencies; antioxidant supplements; oral medicines such as pentoxifylline, which aims to improve blood flow; and a course of <strong>injections</strong> into the bands, commonly a corticosteroid with or without the enzyme hyaluronidase, given at intervals over several weeks. These measures can ease symptoms and improve opening in some people, but results vary and are often modest in advanced disease. Injections can cause temporary soreness, and the long-term benefit of several of these treatments has not been firmly established.</p>
<h3>4. Surgery for advanced restriction</h3>
<p>When mouth opening is severely limited and other measures have not helped, surgery may be considered. Under general anaesthesia, the surgeon releases or removes the fibrous bands and covers the raw areas with a flap — such as the buccal fat pad from inside the cheek, a nasolabial flap from beside the nose, or tissue from elsewhere in the body — so that they do not simply scar down again. Sometimes the attachment of a chewing muscle to the jaw is also released to free up movement. Our page on <a href="/skin-grafts-and-flaps">skin grafts and flaps</a> explains these reconstructive techniques.</p>

<h2 id="recovery-and-surveillance">Recovery, risks and lifelong surveillance</h2>
<p>After surgery, expect swelling and soreness for one to two weeks and a soft diet — and, crucially, intensive physiotherapy starting within days and continuing for at least several months. Without it, the scar tissue tends to contract again and opening can relapse. Other risks include bleeding, infection, partial loss of a flap, scarring where tissue was taken, and numbness or weakness depending on the technique used. How much opening is gained, and kept, varies between people.</p>
<p>Whatever treatment you have, OSMF needs long-term follow-up — typically every three to six months — with mouth opening measured and the whole mouth examined for new patches or ulcers. Keep up with dental care, because limited opening makes cleaning harder and tooth decay more likely. At Gnathos Facial in Gachibowli, Hyderabad, our <a href="/oral-cancer">oral cancer screening and treatment</a> service covers assessment, biopsy, treatment planning, surgery where needed and long-term surveillance. If cancer is ever diagnosed, our guide to <a href="/blogs/oral-cancer-surgery-and-reconstruction-guide/">oral cancer surgery and reconstruction</a> explains what usually happens next.</p>
